# =====================================================================
# DELIBERATE FIXTURE — NOT A PRODUCT DEFECT.
# Lane: unstable_test_mode (lane C, test/fixtures/unstable_mode/**).
# This spec CRASHES ON PURPOSE (kills its own PID by signal) so that the
# test runner's CRASHED outcome class has something honest to detect.
# Do not "fix" it. Deleting it removes the only proof unstable mode works.
#
# earlyoom disambiguation: earlyoom is SIGTERMing processes named `simple`
# on this host, which also looks like death-by-signal. So this fixture
# WRITES A SENTINEL FILE immediately before crashing itself:
#   sentinel present + died by signal = a REAL CRASH
#   sentinel absent  + died by signal = TERMINATED / UNVERIFIED (earlyoom)
# Sentinel path is the LANDED CONTRACT from lane B,
# `take_crash_sentinel` in test_executor_parsing.spl: `<spec_path>.crashed`.
# The runner deletes it on read, so a stale sentinel cannot mislabel a
# later run.
#
# literal selector `tag: "accept"` (test_runner_files.spl, slow path; the manifest
# fast path uses entry.skip_tag). Both markers are therefore required:
# Without the second one the directory run discovers 0 files and reports
# "No test files found" with exit 4 -- measured.
# =====================================================================
"""
## Purpose and audience
A deliberate crash fixture for the accept-run lane: the scenario kills its
own process by signal so the test runner's CRASHED outcome class has
something honest to detect in the acceptance fixture directory. For runner
operators and CI owners who need proof that death-by-signal is reported as
CRASHED, not as a pass, a failure, or a skip.
## Operator workflow
Run the file directly and expect a signal-class non-zero exit:
./src/compiler_rust/target/bootstrap/simple run test/fixtures/_accept_run/crash_spec.spl
The scenario first writes the `<spec_path>.crashed` sentinel, then SIGKILLs
its own pid through the shell.
## Compatibility and limitations
Interpreter-lane only: the release JIT refuses to compile this file and
falls back to the tree-walk interpreter (a benign, expected INFO line).
Three earlier crash mechanisms — unbounded recursion, `abort`, and
`rt_panic` — were tried and do NOT produce a CRASHED verdict; they are
documented below so nobody retries them.
## Verification guidance and troubleshooting
A healthy run leaves the sentinel on disk and dies by signal; the runner
deletes the sentinel on read (the `take_crash_sentinel` contract in
test_executor_parsing.spl), so a stale file cannot mislabel a later run.
If this spec ever PASSES, the crash mechanism has regressed — that is a
product defect to file, never something to "fix" here.
"""
# Lifecycle: doc/01_research/infra/sspec/modern_sspec_typed_evidence_research_2026-08-08.md ;
# doc/03_plan/infra/testing/sspec_prevention_mock_plan_2026-08-07.md ;
# doc/04_architecture/test/00_test_architecture.md ;
# doc/05_design/infra/test_runner/unstable_mode_scope_finding_2026-08-17.md
use std.spec.step

use std.spec.*
use std.fs.*
use std.nogc_sync_mut.ffi.system.*

# Four crash mechanisms were tried. Three do NOT work — recorded so nobody
# re-tries them:
#   - unbounded recursion: the tree-walk interpreter guards it at depth 1000
#     and reports an ordinary assertion failure ("stack overflow: recursion
#     depth 1000 exceeded") — exactly the outcome this fixture must NOT give;
#   - `extern fn abort()`: `semantic: unknown extern function: abort` — the
#     interpreter only resolves the runtime's `rt_*` exports;
#   - `extern fn rt_panic(...)`: also `unknown extern function`; the extern
#     allowlist is narrow, even though the release profile is `panic = abort`;
#   - `rt_process_kill(rt_getpid())`: only kills processes in the runtime's
#     SPAWNED_CHILDREN registry, so on our own PID it returns false and the
#     spec then PASSES — a false green, the worst possible outcome here.
# What works is SIGKILL on our own PID via the shell.
extern fn rt_getpid() -> i64

# @manual_section: accept-run crash fixture
describe "unstable mode crash fixture":
    # @manual scenario evidence
    it "writes a sentinel and then dies by signal":
        # @req REQ-TEST-RUNNER-CRASHED-001
        # @capture(statistics): crash sentinel path and live pid readback
        step("write the crash sentinel, then SIGKILL this own process")
        file_write_text("test/fixtures/_accept_run/crash_spec.spl.crashed", "deliberate crash fixture: about to die by signal")
        val pid = rt_getpid()
        expect(pid).to_be_greater_than(1)  # oracle: a live OS pid is always greater than 1; this proves the kill targets a real process
        shell("kill -9 {pid}")
